(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-11-03"] = {"mth-11-03-m01":{"idea":"A circle is all points at a fixed distance r from its centre. With the centre at the origin, the equation is very short.","steps":["Take a point (x, y) on the circle.","Its distance from the origin is √(x² + y²).","This distance must equal r, so √(x² + y²) = r.","Square both sides: x² + y² = r²."],"others":"x² − y² = r² is a hyperbola, not a circle. x + y = r is a straight line. x² + y² = r has r where r² should be, so its units are wrong.","trap":"The right side is r², not r.","answer":"x² + y² = r²"},"mth-11-03-m02":{"idea":"The centre is now (h, k). We measure the distance from (x, y) to this centre and set it equal to r.","steps":["The distance from (x, y) to (h, k) is √((x − h)² + (y − k)²).","Set it equal to r.","Square both sides: (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r².","Example: centre (2, −3), r = 4 gives (x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 16."],"others":"A minus between the squares gives a hyperbola. The product (x − h)(y − k) = r² also gives a hyperbola. Plus signs inside the brackets would put the centre at (−h, −k).","trap":"Inside the brackets, the signs are opposite to the centre coordinates.","answer":"(x − h)² + (y − k)² = r²"},"mth-11-03-m03":{"idea":"To find the centre from the general equation, complete the squares in x and in y.","steps":["Start with x² + y² + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0.","x² + 2gx = (x + g)² − g², and y² + 2fy = (y + f)² − f².","So (x + g)² + (y + f)² = g² + f² − c.","Compare with (x − h)² + (y − k)²: the centre is (−g, −f)."],"others":"(g, f) has the wrong signs. (−f, −g) swaps the letters. (2g, 2f) uses the whole coefficients, not the half coefficients with opposite sign.","trap":"Take half of the x coefficient and change its sign.","answer":"(−g, −f)"},"mth-11-03-m04":{"idea":"After completing the squares, the right side is the square of the radius.","steps":["We get (x + g)² + (y + f)² = g² + f² − c.","The right side equals r².","So r = √(g² + f² − c ).","This only works when g² + f² − c is positive."],"others":"g + f − c is not what completing squares gives. Without the square root, g² + f² − c would be r², not r. With + c, the sign of the constant is wrong.","trap":"Note the minus before c.","answer":"√(g² + f² − c)"},"mth-11-03-m05":{"idea":"We compare the given equation with the general form to read g, f and c.","steps":["2g = −6, so g = −3. 2f = 8, so f = 4. c = −11.","The centre is (−g, −f) = (3, −4).","The radius is √(g² + f² − c ) = √(9 + 16 + 11).","That is √36 = 6."],"others":"(6, −8) uses the full coefficients, not their halves. The centre (−3, 4) has the wrong signs. The radius 36 forgets the square root.","trap":"Radius is the square root, not r².","answer":"(3, −4) and 6"},"mth-11-03-m06":{"idea":"A real circle needs a positive radius squared. We check g² + f² − c first.","steps":["Compare: 2g = 2, 2f = 2, so g = 1, f = 1, and c = 5.","Radius squared is g² + f² − c = 1 + 1 − 5 = −3.","A square cannot be negative for a real radius.","So there is no real circle."],"others":"A circle of radius 1 or 5 needs radius squared of 1 or 25, but here it is −3. A single point needs radius squared of exactly 0, not −3.","trap":"If g² + f² − c is negative, stop. There is no real circle.","answer":"no real circle, since g² + f² − c is negative"},"mth-11-03-m07":{"idea":"If AB is a diameter, any point P on the circle sees AB at a right angle. This gives the equation.","steps":["The angle APB is 90°, so PA is perpendicular to PB.","Their dot product is zero: (x − x₁)(x − x₂) + (y − y₁)(y − y₂) = 0.","Check A and B: each makes one factor zero.","So the equation holds at both end points."],"others":"A mix of (x − x₁)(y − y₁) terms does not come from the dot product. Plus signs inside the brackets use the wrong points. A minus between the products gives a hyperbola.","trap":"Pair x with x and y with y, and add.","answer":"(x − x₁)(x − x₂) + (y − y₁)(y − y₂) = 0"},"mth-11-03-m08":{"idea":"For a circle with a given diameter, the centre is the mid-point and the radius is half of the diameter.","steps":["Centre = ((2 + 6)/2, (3 + 7)/2) = (4, 5).","Diameter = √((6 − 2)² + (7 − 3)²) = √32 = 4√2.","Radius = half of this = 2√2.","So centre (4, 5) and radius 2√2."],"others":"(8, 10) is the sum of the coordinates, not the mid-point. (2, 2) with radius 4 is the vector between the points. Radius 8 is far too large, since the diameter is only 5.66.","trap":"Mid-point means average, not sum.","answer":"(4, 5) and 2√2"},"mth-11-03-m09":{"idea":"The tangent at a point is perpendicular to the radius to that point. This gives a neat form.","steps":["The radius to (x₁, y₁) has direction (x₁, y₁).","A point (x, y) of the tangent satisfies (x − x₁)x₁ + (y − y₁)y₁ = 0.","So xx₁ + yy₁ = x₁² + y₁² = r².","Example: x² + y² = 25 at (3, 4) gives 3x + 4y = 25."],"others":"x + y = r² has no x₁ or y₁. The version with a minus sign is a hyperbola's tangent. The version with r on the right is not dimensionally correct: it needs r².","trap":"Replace x² by xx₁ and y² by yy₁ in the circle equation.","answer":"xx₁ + yy₁ = r²"},"mth-11-03-m10":{"idea":"A point on the circle can be written as (r cos θ, r sin θ). We put it into the tangent formula.","steps":["Use xx₁ + yy₁ = r² with x₁ = r cos θ and y₁ = r sin θ.","This gives x r cos θ + y r sin θ = r².","Divide by r: x cos θ + y sin θ = r.","This is the normal form with p = r."],"others":"Sine and cosine swapped would give a tangent at a different point. A minus sign gives a tangent at (r cos θ, −r sin θ). Right side r² forgets to divide by r.","trap":"Check at θ = 0: the tangent at (r, 0) is x = r.","answer":"x cos θ + y sin θ = r"},"mth-11-03-m11":{"idea":"We find the tangent at (3, 4) and then set y = 0 to see where it meets the x axis.","steps":["The tangent at (3, 4) to x² + y² = 25 is 3x + 4y = 25.","Put y = 0: 3x = 25.","So x = 25/3, about 8.33.","The point is (25/3, 0)."],"others":"(4, 0) and (3, 0) are just the feet of the point's coordinates, not where the tangent meets the axis. (5, 0) is where the circle meets the axis, but the tangent at (3, 4) is a different line.","trap":"The tangent meets the axis outside the circle, so x must exceed 5.","answer":"(25/3, 0)"},"mth-11-03-m12":{"idea":"The tangent length is found with Pythagoras in a right triangle: centre, point of contact, and the outside point.","steps":["For a point (x₁, y₁), the tangent length is √(x₁² + y₁² − r²).","Here x₁ = 5, y₁ = 0, r² = 9.","Length = √(25 + 0 − 9) = √16.","So the length is 4."],"others":"5 is the distance of the point from the centre, not the tangent length. 16 forgets the square root. 3 is the radius.","trap":"It is a 3-4-5 triangle: radius 3, tangent 4, hypotenuse 5.","answer":"4"},"mth-11-03-m13":{"idea":"A line touches a circle when its distance from the centre equals the radius.","steps":["Write the line as mx − y + c = 0.","Its distance from the origin is |c|/√(m² + 1).","Set this equal to r: |c|/√(1 + m²) = r.","Square: c² = r²(1 + m²)."],"others":"c² = r² + m has the wrong power of m. c² = r²(1 − m²) has a wrong sign. c = r(1 + m²) is not squared.","trap":"This is the tangent condition. Learn it with the 1 + m² factor.","answer":"c² = r²(1 + m²)"},"mth-11-03-m14":{"idea":"We use the tangent condition with slope m = 1 and r = 4.","steps":["The condition is c² = r²(1 + m²).","Here r² = 16 and m = 1.","c² = 16 × 2 = 32.","So c = ±√32 = ±4√2."],"others":"±8 is double the radius. ±4 would be the answer for a horizontal line y = c. ±16 is the value of r².","trap":"Check: the distance of the origin from x − y + 4√2 = 0 is 4√2/√2 = 4.","answer":"±4√2"},"mth-11-03-m15":{"idea":"For a given slope, the tangent condition gives c² = r²(1 + m²), and this has two solutions for c.","steps":["c² is fixed, so c has two values: positive and negative.","Each value gives a line with slope m.","The lines have the same slope, so they are parallel.","They lie on opposite sides of the centre."],"others":"Four tangents is wrong, since c has only two values. One tangent would need c = 0 to be the only root. Zero tangents is wrong, as every direction has a tangent.","trap":"Think of two parallel lines touching the circle on opposite sides.","answer":"two, and they are parallel to each other"},"mth-11-03-m16":{"idea":"The director circle is the set of points from which the two tangents are at a right angle.","steps":["From an outside point P, draw two tangents.","If they are at 90°, then P, the two touch points and the centre make a square.","The side of the square is r, so the diagonal OP is r√2.","So the director circle is the locus of such points P."],"others":"From a point on the circle only one tangent exists, but that is not the director circle. Parallel tangents never come from a single point. A distance r from every tangent describes the centre.","trap":"Director circle means perpendicular tangents.","answer":"from which the two tangents to the circle are perpendicular to each other"},"mth-11-03-m17":{"idea":"The director circle is centred at the same point as the circle, with radius r√2.","steps":["A point P(x, y) on the director circle is at distance r√2 from the origin.","So x² + y² = (r√2)².","That is x² + y² = 2r².","The radius is r√2."],"others":"x² + y² = r² is the circle itself. 4r² would give radius 2r, and r²/2 gives radius r/√2. Neither makes the tangents perpendicular.","trap":"Square r√2 to get 2r².","answer":"x² + y² = 2r²"},"mth-11-03-m18":{"idea":"The radius of the director circle is r√2 for a circle of radius r.","steps":["The circle x² + y² = 25 has r = 5.","The director circle has radius r√2 = 5√2.","5√2 is about 7.07.","Check: the point (5, 5) is at distance √50 = 5√2."],"others":"10 is the diameter. 5.0 is the radius of the circle itself. 25 is r².","trap":"The director circle is bigger than the circle by a factor of √2.","answer":"5√2"},"mth-11-03-m19":{"idea":"A perpendicular from the centre to a chord cuts the chord in half. Then Pythagoras gives half of the chord.","steps":["The distance from the centre to the line is p.","In the right triangle, the radius r is the hypotenuse and p is one side.","Half chord = √(r² − p²).","Full chord = 2√(r² − p²)."],"others":"2(r − p) is not a Pythagoras result. √(r² − p²) is only half the chord. √(r² + p²) is the distance from the centre to the chord end for a different triangle.","trap":"Example: r = 5, p = 3, chord = 2 × 4 = 8.","answer":"2√(r² − p²)"},"mth-11-03-m20":{"idea":"A line and a circle can meet in 0, 1 or 2 points. The distance from the centre decides which.","steps":["Compare the distance d from the centre with the radius r.","If d < r, the line cuts at two points.","If d = r, the line touches at one point.","Here d = 5 = r, so the line is a tangent."],"others":"A diameter passes through the centre, so its distance is 0. A secant needs d less than 5. A line that misses the circle needs d greater than 5.","trap":"Equal distance and radius means tangent.","answer":"a tangent to the circle"},"mth-11-03-m21":{"idea":"A right angle at the origin means the opposite segment is a diameter. This avoids a long calculation.","steps":["The points (0, 0), (4, 0), (0, 6) make a right angle at the origin.","So the segment from (4, 0) to (0, 6) is a diameter.","Its length is √(16 + 36) = √52 = 2√13.","The radius is half: √13."],"others":"√26 is half of √52 taken twice. 5 and 13 do not come from any step. 2√13 is the diameter, not the radius.","trap":"Radius is half of the diameter.","answer":"√13"},"mth-11-03-n01":{"idea":"We read g, f and c from the equation and use the radius formula.","steps":["2g = −6 gives g = −3. 2f = 8 gives f = 4. c = −11.","r = √(g² + f² − c ).","r = √(9 + 16 + 11) = √36.","So r = 6.000."],"trap":"Do not forget that c is negative, so −c adds 11.","answer":"6.000"},"mth-11-03-n02":{"idea":"The angle at the origin is 90°, so the segment joining the other two points is a diameter.","steps":["Diameter = √((4 − 0)² + (0 − 6)²) = √52.","√52 = 7.211.","Radius = 7.211/2.","Radius = 3.606."],"trap":"Check: the centre (2, 3) is at distance √13 = 3.606 from the origin.","answer":"3.606"},"mth-11-03-n03":{"idea":"The tangent length uses Pythagoras: it is the square root of the power of the point.","steps":["Length = √(x₁² + y₁² − r²).","Here x₁ = 5, y₁ = 0, r² = 9.","Length = √(25 − 9) = √16.","So the length is 4.000."],"trap":"Do not forget the square root.","answer":"4.000"},"mth-11-03-n04":{"idea":"We write the tangent at (3, 4) and put y = 0.","steps":["The tangent at (x₁, y₁) is xx₁ + yy₁ = 25.","Here it is 3x + 4y = 25.","Put y = 0: 3a = 25.","a = 25/3 = 8.333."],"trap":"The answer must be greater than 5, because the tangent meets the axis outside the circle.","answer":"8.333"},"mth-11-03-n05":{"idea":"A line touches the circle when c² = r²(1 + m²). We take the positive value of c.","steps":["The slope is m = 1 and r² = 16.","c² = 16(1 + 1) = 32.","c = √32 = 4√2.","c = 5.657."],"trap":"Check: the distance of the origin from x − y + c = 0 is c/√2 = 4.","answer":"5.657"},"mth-11-03-n06":{"idea":"The director circle has the same centre and a radius of r√2.","steps":["The circle x² + y² = 25 has r = 5.","Director circle: x² + y² = 2r² = 50.","Its radius is √50 = 5√2.","So the radius is 7.071."],"trap":"Check: 5 × 1.4142 = 7.071.","answer":"7.071"},"mth-11-03-n07":{"idea":"The perpendicular from the centre cuts the chord in half. Pythagoras gives the half chord.","steps":["r² = 25 and p = 3, so p² = 9.","Half chord = √(25 − 9) = √16 = 4.","Full chord = 2 × 4.","So the chord is 8.000."],"trap":"Check with y = 3: the points are (−4, 3) and (4, 3), 8 apart.","answer":"8.000"},"mth-11-03-n08":{"idea":"The end points of a diameter give the radius as half of their distance.","steps":["Diameter = √((6 − 2)² + (7 − 3)²) = √(16 + 16).","That is √32 = 5.657.","Radius = 5.657/2.","Radius = 2.828."],"trap":"Check: the equation (x − 2)(x − 6) + (y − 3)(y − 7) = 0 gives r = √8 = 2.828.","answer":"2.828"}};
